// Crew members: the people who work the hours but do not log in.
//
// Part of the multi-file Prisma schema in this folder.
//
// SHIPS DELIBERATELY UNWIRED. Nothing in the application reads or writes
// CrewMember yet, and TimeEntry.crewMemberId is NULL on every row. That is
// the exception CLAUDE.md's "written, documented, and never called" rule
// warns about, taken on purpose and stated here so the next reader does not
// mistake it for the accident: the structure is the half that needs a
// migration against real production data and a Slack announcement, and the
// wiring is the half that can be reverted with a revert. They are separated
// so the irreversible half lands alone and small.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS.
//
//   TimeEntry.employeeUserId is a required foreign key to User, and User
//   carries a globally-unique clerkId and a globally-unique email. So an
//   hour can only be recorded against somebody who has completed a Clerk
//   sign-up. A framing sub with a 25-hand crew therefore needs 25 Clerk
//   accounts before it can record one hour, and certified payroll, fringe
//   remittance, the apprentice ratio check and job labour cost all read
//   TimeEntry. That is not a small friction; it is the reason the labour
//   half of this product cannot be used by the trade it was built for.
//
//   A carpenter does not need an inbox. They need to be NAMED, correctly
//   and permanently, on a document their employer signs and files with a
//   government agency.
//
// WHY NOT A User THAT CANNOT SIGN IN. It would reuse every existing
// foreign key and every read path, which is genuinely tempting. It was
// rejected on three counts, all of them structural:
//
//   1. User.clerkId and User.email are BOTH globally unique. Twenty-five
//      crew members need twenty-five synthetic clerkIds and twenty-five
//      synthetic emails, in columns where a collision is a hard failure
//      and where the uniqueness is global rather than per-company.
//   2. requireCompanyContext() adopts a User row found by a VERIFIED
//      email (see apps/web/lib/auth.ts, and the Clerk entry in CLAUDE.md).
//      A placeholder address that somebody later registers for real would
//      inherit that company. The unique-email rule is load-bearing
//      security, and filling it with invented values is exactly the
//      cross-tenant problem it exists to prevent.
//   3. A User is enumerated everywhere — the team page, job assignment,
//      permissions, notification dispatch. A non-signing User would wire
//      itself into all of them by existing, which is the opposite of what
//      this change is trying to do.
//
// The cost of a separate model is honest and is paid later: every place
// that names a worker will need to read one of two identities. That cost
// is bounded (five modules, listed in the report) and it buys an identity
// record that cannot be confused with an account.

/// One person on the crew who has no login — the identity a filed
/// certified payroll names.
///
/// This is an EVIDENCE record in the sense CLAUDE.md uses the word:
/// identity fields are locked after creation, and a row is archived rather
/// than deleted. Once a WH-347 naming this person has been signed and
/// filed, the row it was built from has to keep saying the same thing. The
/// lock is a database trigger (see the migration), not a convention in an
/// action, because a convention is precisely what an unwired model has no
/// way to enforce.
model CrewMember {
  id        String  @id @default(cuid())
  companyId String
  company   Company @relation(fields: [companyId], references: [id])

  /// The legal name, in three fields rather than one, because that is how
  /// a payroll form is laid out and because splitting a single string back
  /// into parts is a guess that fails on the first two-word surname.
  /// LOCKED after creation by trigger. A misspelling is corrected by
  /// archiving the row and creating a new one, which leaves both the old
  /// filing and the correction on the record — a silent UPDATE would make
  /// a filed document and its source disagree with nothing to show for it.
  legalFirstName  String
  legalMiddleName String?
  legalLastName   String

  /// The LAST FOUR DIGITS of the SSN, and nothing more.
  ///
  /// WH-347 column 1E wants an "individual identifying number", and the
  /// Department of Labor's instructions accept EITHER the last four digits
  /// of the SSN OR any number specific to the individual worker — and say
  /// plainly that the full SSN must not be included.
  /// (https://www.dol.gov/agencies/whd/forms/wh347)
  ///
  /// That is why there are two columns, not one. This one is the SSN-derived
  /// identifier and is CHECKed in the migration to exactly four digits,
  /// which is what stops somebody pasting a whole SSN into it — the full
  /// number is deliberately not a column anywhere in this schema, because a
  /// column that could hold it would eventually hold it.
  /// `employeeNumber` below is the other permitted form, the contractor's
  /// own worker-specific number. Whichever exists is what a future filing
  /// prints; a company that uses badge numbers never has to supply an SSN
  /// digit to this app at all.
  ///
  /// Nullable because a crew member is worth recording before payroll has
  /// sent the number over. LOCKED after creation by trigger.
  identifyingNumberLast4 String?

  /// Davis-Bacon's basic-records rule (29 CFR 5.5(a)(3)) requires the
  /// contractor to KEEP the name and address of every labourer and
  /// mechanic, separately from what the weekly WH-347 prints. Recorded here
  /// because the record itself is the obligation. Not locked — an address
  /// genuinely changes, and no filing asserts it.
  addressLine1 String?
  addressLine2 String?
  city         String?
  state        String?
  zip          String?

  phone String?

  /// The payroll/badge number the contractor already uses, if any — and
  /// the OTHER thing WH-347 column 1E accepts as an individual identifying
  /// number, per the note on identifyingNumberLast4 above. Unique per
  /// company where present; Postgres treats NULLs as distinct, so any
  /// number of crew members can have none. This is the only identifier
  /// that can safely be unique — a name cannot, because two real people
  /// share one.
  employeeNumber String?

  /// ENTERED, not stamped, like every date that matters in this schema.
  hiredOn DateTime?

  /// Set WHEN AND IF this person later completes a real sign-up — see the
  /// report's answer on transitions. It is a forward pointer that says
  /// "this login is the same human as this crew record". It is NOT a
  /// redirect: nothing about a historical TimeEntry changes when it is
  /// set, because every read of a crew member's name reads the locked
  /// fields above rather than the mutable User.name. Unique, so two crew
  /// records can never claim one login, and locked once set by the same
  /// trigger, so a login cannot be moved from one person's history to
  /// another's.
  linkedUserId String?   @unique
  linkedUser   User?     @relation(fields: [linkedUserId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  linkedAt     DateTime?

  /// Left the crew. There is no delete: a person named on a filed payroll
  /// cannot stop having existed. The relation from TimeEntry is
  /// onDelete: Restrict for the same reason — the database refuses to
  /// orphan the identity behind an hour that has been certified.
  archivedAt DateTime?

  note String?

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  timeEntries TimeEntry[]

  @@unique([companyId, employeeNumber])
  @@index([companyId])
  @@index([archivedAt])
}
